
“That is the point.”




He took the mouse and moved the playhead back. Maren let him. He restored the sound and played from the car. His recorded voice emerged beneath the engine noise, low and uneven. He called the sound a scream, corrected himself, described the break in the middle, and said it stopped when he answered. Twice he asked Maren to turn the camera off. Twice, several minutes later, he asked her to turn it on again.




He stopped at the passenger door.




“That is context,” he said.




“It is also private.”




“It explains why I left the car.”




“No. It records what you said before you left the car.”




“You think there’s a difference because the camera missed the middle.”




Maren did not answer.




Jonah leaned back. The chair wheels struck a cable guard beneath the desk. “Then we go back and record the middle properly.”




Maren slid the printed conditions between them. “One visit. We leave before one. We stay on the marked routes. We call Lena when we enter, at ten, at midnight, and when we leave. No solo work. No emitter. We keep continuous audio, but either of us can stop a camera pointed at us. This card stays separate. After the investigation, you watch it again before anything from it enters the episode.”




He read the page without touching it.




“I can change my mind later,” he said.




“Yes.”




“Even if I agree now.”




“Yes.”




“And you can tell people I changed my mind.”




“No.”




“You can say the footage exists.”




“Only if you agree.”




“You can build an episode around a hole you refuse to show.”




“I can cancel the episode.”




He looked up. “You would do that?”




Maren closed the editing program. The project disappeared behind the desktop folders. “Yes.”




The air conditioner shut off. Chargers hummed along the shelves. Somewhere beyond the office wall, a cart rolled across the warehouse floor below them, four hard wheel contacts followed by a softer drag.




Jonah turned toward the sound.




Maren waited until he looked back.




He tapped the first line on the page. “Do we have permission?”




“I have a call at three.”




“You scheduled the call before we agreed.”




“I scheduled it before I knew whether I would cancel.”




He read the remaining lines. “No solo sessions means we can’t test whether it’s following me.”




“It means you don’t leave my sight.”




“That changes the conditions.”




“Good.”




“It could change the result.”




“Good.”




He pushed the paper back. “Eight rules change what we can do out there.”




“They’re supposed to.”




“That changes what we might get.”




“And it gives us eight ways to leave with the same number of people we entered with.”




The office phone rang before he could answer. Maren put the preserve contact on speaker and opened the map attachment already waiting in her email.




The woman introduced herself as Ruth Halvorsen, the preserve manager. She spoke from an office with another phone ringing in the background and stopped twice to answer someone away from the receiver. Maren stated their arrival time, planned departure, equipment, and purpose. She did not mention an attachment, a protector, or a grave.




“You can film the shelter and the marked trail loop after hours,” Halvorsen said. “The vehicle gate will be locked behind you. Your driver needs to meet you outside it at the agreed time.”




“We’ll have a satellite messenger and two phones,” Maren said.




“Cell service drops along the river. Use the messenger for an emergency, not for routine check-ins. Do not leave the trail to improve reception.”




Jonah leaned toward the phone. “The article we sent mentioned a grave west of the shelter.”




“There is no public feature under that name.”




“Public feature,” Jonah repeated.




Halvorsen paused. “There are protected archaeological areas in the preserve. Their locations are not supplied for recreational filming. You do not enter them, move stone, place equipment on structures, or follow unofficial paths.”




“We won’t,” Maren said.




Jonah took the printed article from beneath a camera manual. A red circle marked a point beyond the trail loop. “The coordinates in this piece—”




“Are not preserve coordinates,” Halvorsen said. “That page combines several places. The stone shelter is a public structure from the nineteen-thirties. The crime people ask about did not occur inside it. The settlement remnants, quarry features, and protected sites are not one location.”




Maren enlarged the official map on the second monitor. The shelter stood near the entrance loop. The river crossing lay farther north. Hatched protection zones appeared without interior detail. The red circle from Jonah’s printout fell outside the preserve boundary when she aligned the roads.




Jonah moved closer to the screen. “That can’t be right.”




“It is the map supplied by the state,” Maren said.




“The article has photographs.”




“Photographs can be labeled incorrectly,” Halvorsen said. “I need you to confirm that you understand the access conditions.”




Maren read them back. Marked trails. No entry into protected zones. No moving or touching archaeological material. No digging, marking, climbing, or leaving equipment. Entry at seven. Exit call no later than twelve forty-five. The manager would record their vehicle, driver, and emergency contact.




Jonah remained silent until Halvorsen asked for his confirmation.




“I understand,” he said.




“Both of you will sign the permit when you arrive. Bring a printed route plan. Weather changes quickly near the river. If either of you becomes disoriented, injured, or unable to continue, leave by the shortest marked route and contact us after you are outside the gate.”




Maren wrote the sentence at the bottom of her page.




After the call ended, Jonah studied the two maps. He placed the article over the official printout, shifted it, rotated it, and aligned the river bends. No position made the red circle fall on the shelter.




“The sound still happened,” he said.




“I know.”




“The map being wrong doesn’t make the sound wrong.”




“No.”




“It doesn’t explain the notebook.”




“No.”




He looked at her. “You’re different when the camera’s on.”




Maren looked at him.




She turned to the equipment shelves.




Maren took the broken field meter from the top equipment bin and set it between them. Its display was dark. She opened the battery compartment, pressed the loose contact into place, and switched it on. The numbers climbed, dropped to zero, then jumped when she flexed the casing.




“This was in your list,” she said.




“It worked at the shelter.”




“It changes when I squeeze it.”




“It changed there without anyone touching it.”




“We don’t have a baseline from there. We didn’t log the generator. We didn’t record where our phones were. We did not even bring the audio recorder.”




“We were scouting.”




“And now we are not.”




She switched the meter off and placed it in a bin labeled REPAIR. Beside it lay the electromagnetic emitter: a black box with a wire coil, toggle switch, and a strip of white tape bearing Jonah’s handwriting. H.R. TEST.




Maren removed it and set it on the upper shelf.




“No emitter,” she said.




Jonah watched her. “Why?”




“Because it can interfere with equipment we’re bringing.”
